An optimal and elegant way to solve this problem is to use a **Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree)**.

Since the values of elements are bounded by $1 \le num \le 10^5$, we can maintain:
1. A frequency Fenwick tree (`bitCount`) to store the count of each number currently in the last $m$ elements.
2. A sum Fenwick tree (`bitSum`) to store the sum of each number currently in the last $m$ elements.

Using **binary lifting** on the Fenwick tree, we can compute the sum of the smallest $X$ elements in $O(\log(\max(num)))$ time. 
The sum of elements after removing the smallest $k$ and largest $k$ elements is simply:
$$\text{sumSmallest}(m - k) - \text{sumSmallest}(k)$$

Dividing this by $m - 2k$ (rounded down) gives the answer in $O(\log(\max(num)))$ time per query.

