An optimal strategy to maximize the binary string comes from analyzing the two operations:
1. `"10" -> "01"`: This allows any `'0'` to move to the left past a `'1'`. That means all `'0'`s that appear after the first `'0'` can be moved leftward until all `'0'`s form a single contiguous block.
2. `"00" -> "10"`: A block of $k$ contiguous zeros can be transformed step-by-step into $k - 1$ ones followed by a single zero (`"00...0"` $\to$ `"11...10"`).

Since neither operation allows a `'0'` to move to the right past a `'1'`, and neither operation can eliminate the last `'0'`, any string with at least two `'0'`s can always be transformed into a string containing exactly one `'0'`, placed as far right as possible.

Specifically:
- If the string has fewer than two `'0'`s, no `"00" -> "10"` operation can be performed, so the string cannot be improved and we return it as is.
- Otherwise, let `first_zero` be the index of the first `'0'`, and let `zeros` be the total count of `'0'`s in the string.
- All zeros can be grouped starting from `first_zero`. After applying `"00" -> "10"` repeatedly, the only remaining `'0'` will be placed at index `first_zero + zeros - 1`.
- Every other character in the resulting string will be `'1'`.

### Implementation

```typescript
function maximumBinaryString(binary: string): string {
    const n = binary.length;
    let zeros = 0;
    let firstZero = -1;

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (binary[i] === '0') {
            zeros++;
            if (firstZero === -1) {
                firstZero = i;
            }
        }
    }

    if (zeros <= 1) {
        return binary;
    }

    const res = new Array(n).fill('1');
    res[firstZero + zeros - 1] = '0';
    return res.join('');
}
