An elegant and optimal solution in TypeScript using recursion with memoization (DP).

### Method Explanation

In each round with $k$ players, the $i$-th player from the front competes against the $(k - i + 1)$-th player from the front for $i = 1, \dots, \lfloor k/2 \rfloor$. If $k$ is odd, the middle player advances automatically.

When `firstPlayer` ($p_1$) and `secondPlayer` ($p_2$) face each other (i.e., $p_1 + p_2 = k + 1$), the tournament for our question ends in the current round.

Otherwise, both $p_1$ and $p_2$ are guaranteed to win their matches and advance to the next round. For any other match, either player can win. Since players are re-indexed based on their original relative order, their new positions in the next round will simply be:
- $p'_1 = 1 + (\text{number of winners with index } < p_1)$
- $p'_2 = 1 + (\text{number of winners with index } < p_2)$

Because $n \le 28$, the number of pairs in any round is at most $14$. We can determine all possible pairs of new positions $(p'_1, p'_2)$ by iterating through all matches and maintaining the set of reachable counts of winners before $p_1$ and $p_2$. We then recursively solve for the next round and use memoization on the state `(k, p1, p2)`.

